  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef and Chefina are playing a game of Tetris together. In a single move, a player can either clear 111, 222, 333, or 444 lines of blocks. Clearing 444 lines in a single move is called getting a \"Tetris\".\n\nChef and Chefina take turns playing, with Chef starting first.\n\nWhenever the current player clears only one line, the turn shifts to the other player; otherwise it remains with the current player.\n\nThey will stop playing as soon as **at least** LLL lines are cleared in total. That is, the game stops when the sum of the number of lines cleared by both the players is at least LLL.\n\nChef wants to end the game in style, and so would like to be the one who finishes the game and do so by getting a \"Tetris\", i.e, by clearing 444 lines.\n\nHow many sequences of moves are there in which this happens?\n\nThe number of sequences may be large, so print it modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach of the next TTT lines contains one integer LLL — the number of lines to be cleared.\n\n\nOutput Format\n\nFor each test case, output on a new line the total number of ways in which Chef can finish the game by getting a \"Tetris\", modulo 109+710^9+7109+7.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤L≤1051 \\leq L \\leq 10^51≤L≤105"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n3\n2\n4\n100000",
        "output": "3\n1\n4\n246691813",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: For L=3L=3L=3, there are 333 possible ways such that Chef can finish the game with a \"Tetris\":\n\nClear 444 lines immediately.\nClear 222 lines first, then 444.\nClear one line, transferring the turn to Chefina. Chefina then clears one line, transferring the turn back to Chef, who then gets a \"Tetris\".\n\nTest case 222: For L=2L = 2L=2, the only possibility is that Chef gets a \"Tetris\" on the very first move.\nTest case 333: For L=4L = 4L=4, there are 444 valid sequences of moves: (2,4),(1,1,4),(3,4),(4)(2,4), (1,1,4), (3,4), (4)(2,4),(1,1,4),(3,4),(4)."
      }
    ]
  },
